      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that the first card drawn is a 6 and the second card drawn is a Queen from a standard deck of 52 cards, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 as the first card.**\n2. **Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen as the second card given that a 6 has already been drawn.**\n3. **Multiply these probabilities together to find the overall probability.**\n\n### Step 1: Probability of drawing a 6 as the first card\n\nA standard deck has 52 cards, with 4 cards of each suit (hearts, clubs, spades, and diamonds).\n\nThe number of 6s in the deck is:\n\\[ 4 \\text{ (since there are 4 sixes in each suit)} \\]\n\nThe probability of drawing a 6 as the first card is:\n\\[ P(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Probability of drawing a Queen as the second card given that a 6 has already been drawn\n\nAfter drawing a 6, there are now 51 cards left in the deck. There are still 4 Queen cards in the deck.\n\nThe probability of drawing a Queen as the second card, given that a 6 has already been drawn, is:\n\\[ P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the overall probability\n\nThe overall probability of both events happening (drawing a 6 first and then a Queen) is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\\[ P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen}) = P(\\text{First card is a 6}) \\times P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{1}{13} \\times \\frac{4}{51} \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{4}{663} \\]\n\nThus, the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}} \\]",
